`g` is passed to `helper`, a generic function: a guard is handed only to a function named at the call, neither a method, a constructor nor a generic, whose parameter is declared as the guard is, `MutexGuard<T>`, once per call, because that parameter is held to the rules of the guard's block; write the helper as `const useGuard = (g: MutexGuard<Counter>): void => { console.log(g.value.total) }` and call it as `useGuard(g)`
